111	Desert Willow	Chilopsis linearis	Deciduous Tree	The Desert Willow is a medium-sized tree known for its beautiful, trumpet-shaped flowers and slender, willow-like leaves. It is native to the southwestern United States and Mexico, thriving in arid environments. The tree typically reaches a height of 15 to 30 feet and has a spreading canopy.		Full sun	Low to moderate; drought tolerant once established	10 to 15 feet apart	15 to 30 feet tall and 10 to 20 feet wide	Typically blooms from late spring to summer	Easy	Desert Willows prefer well-drained soils and are highly drought-resistant. Watering should be infrequent but deep, allowing the soil to dry out between watering. Pruning is recommended to maintain shape and remove any dead or crossing branches. Fertilization is generally not necessary but can be done in the spring with a balanced fertilizer if desired.	f	t	f	f		drought-tolerant, flowering tree, native plant	Bignoniaceae	Desert Willow can be propagated by seed or cuttings. Seeds should be scarified and soaked before planting to improve germination rates. Softwood cuttings taken in the spring can also root successfully.		flower_garden	loam
