120	Laceleaf Maple	Acer palmatum var. dissectum	Deciduous tree	The Laceleaf Maple, also known as the Japanese Maple, is a small to medium-sized tree characterized by its finely dissected, lace-like leaves that offer a delicate appearance. The tree typically exhibits vibrant fall colors ranging from deep reds to bright yellows. It is often used in landscaping for its aesthetic appeal and graceful branching structure.		Partial shade to full sun	Regular watering, keep soil moist but not soggy	5 to 10 feet apart, depending on the variety	Typically 4 to 10 feet tall and wide	Spring through fall	Moderate	Laceleaf Maples prefer well-drained, slightly acidic soil. They thrive in areas with protection from strong winds and harsh sunlight. Regular pruning is recommended to maintain shape and remove any dead or damaged branches. Fertilize in early spring with a balanced fertilizer to promote healthy growth.	f	f	f	f		ornamental, deciduous, landscaping, Japanese Maple	Aceraceae	Can be propagated through softwood cuttings, grafting, or seed, though grafting is the most common method for maintaining specific cultivars.		flower_garden	loam
121	Chinese Fringe Tree	Chionanthus retusus	Deciduous tree	The Chinese Fringe Tree is a small to medium-sized deciduous tree known for its striking, fringed, white flowers that appear in spring. It features attractive dark green leaves that turn yellow in the fall. The tree typically grows 15-30 feet tall and 15-25 feet wide, making it suitable for smaller landscapes.	https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Chionanthus_retusus3.jpg/960px-Chionanthus_retusus3.jpg	Full sun to partial shade	Moderate; water regularly during dry periods	15-25 feet apart for landscape planting	15-30 feet tall and 15-25 feet wide	Spring for flowers, Fall for foliage color	Moderate	Plant in well-drained soil and ensure adequate space for growth. Water regularly, especially during dry spells. Fertilize in early spring with a balanced fertilizer to promote healthy growth. Prune to maintain shape and remove any dead or diseased branches.	t	f	f	f		ornamental, landscaping, deciduous, flowering	Oleaceae	Propagation is typically done by seed or softwood cuttings.		flower_garden	loam
122	Sweetgum	Liquidambar styraciflua	Deciduous tree	The Sweetgum is a tall deciduous tree native to the eastern United States and parts of Central America. It is characterized by its star-shaped leaves, which turn vibrant shades of red, orange, and yellow in the fall. The tree produces distinctive spiky fruiting bodies that are often referred to as 'gum balls'.	https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/CopalmeDAmerique.jpg/960px-CopalmeDAmerique.jpg	Full sun to partial shade	Moderate; prefers well-drained soil	15-30 feet apart when planting	50-75 feet tall with a spread of 30-50 feet	Spring through fall	Moderate	Sweetgum trees thrive in a variety of soil types but prefer moist, well-drained soils. Regular watering is essential, especially during dry spells. Prune the tree in late winter to remove dead or crossing branches. Fertilization is recommended in early spring with a balanced fertilizer. Watch for pests like aphids and scale, and manage them as necessary.	t	f	f	f		deciduous, tree, ornamental, fall color	Altingiaceae	Can be propagated by seed or by softwood cuttings taken in spring.		flower_garden	loam
123	Yew Tree	Taxus spp.	Conifer	Yew trees are evergreen conifers belonging to the genus Taxus. They are known for their dense, dark green foliage and red, berry-like arils. Yews are often used in landscaping for hedges and topiary due to their ability to be shaped and pruned. The trees can vary in size and form, with some species being small shrubs while others can grow into large trees.	https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Liverpool_Yew_Tree_%282023_ward%29.svg/960px-Liverpool_Yew_Tree_%282023_ward%29.svg.png	Partial to full shade	Moderate; allow soil to dry slightly between waterings	3-10 feet depending on the species and intended use	Varies by species; typically 10-30 feet tall and 10-15 feet wide	Year-round	Moderate	Yews prefer well-drained soil and can tolerate a range of soil types. Regular pruning is recommended to maintain shape and encourage bushy growth. Fertilization in spring with a balanced fertilizer can promote healthy growth. Protect from extreme drought and heavy clay soils. Monitor for pests such as spider mites and scale.	f	f	f	f		evergreen, conifer, landscaping, toxic	Taxaceae	By seed or cuttings; seeds should be stratified before planting.		flower_garden	loam
124	Creeping Thyme	Thymus serpyllum	Perennial herb	Creeping Thyme is a low-growing, mat-forming herb that is often used as a ground cover. It features small, aromatic leaves and produces tiny pink to purple flowers that attract pollinators. This plant is well-known for its ability to thrive in poor soils and dry conditions, making it an excellent choice for rock gardens and pathways.	https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/18/Thymus_serpyllum1.jpg	Full sun to partial shade	Moderate; allow soil to dry out between waterings	12 to 18 inches apart	3 to 6 inches tall and 12 to 24 inches wide	Perennial; blooms from late spring to early summer	Easy	Creeping Thyme prefers well-drained soil and full sun to partial shade. It requires minimal watering once established. Prune back after flowering to promote denser growth and prevent legginess. Fertilize sparingly, as too much fertilizer can reduce flowering.	t	t	f	f		ground cover, aromatic, herb, drought-tolerant, pollinator-friendly	Lamiaceae	Can be propagated by seed, division, or stem cuttings.		flower_garden	loam
125	Coral Pea	Hardenbergia violacea	Perennial vine	Hardenbergia violacea, commonly known as Coral Pea, is a vigorous, twining vine native to Australia. It features glossy, dark green leaves and produces an abundance of purple to blue pea-like flowers that are attractive to pollinators. The plant is often used in gardens for ground cover, trellises, and as an ornamental feature.	https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Wild-growing-Hardenbergia-violacea-2.jpeg/960px-Wild-growing-Hardenbergia-violacea-2.jpeg	Full sun to partial shade	Moderate, allow to dry out between waterings	1-2 feet apart	Up to 10 feet long	Year-round in suitable climates, primarily blooming in late winter to early spring	Easy	Coral Pea prefers well-draining soil and can tolerate drought once established. It thrives in full sun but can also grow in partial shade. Pruning after flowering can help maintain shape and encourage new growth. Fertilizing during the growing season can enhance blooming.	t	f	f	t		vine, perennial, flowering, Australian native	Fabaceae	Seeds or cuttings		flower_garden	loam
126	Monkey Puzzle Tree	Araucaria araucana	Conifer	The Monkey Puzzle Tree, or Araucaria araucana, is a unique evergreen conifer native to Chile and Argentina. Known for its distinctive, spiky foliage and unusual shape, it can grow to heights of up to 50 meters (164 feet). The tree's branches grow in a whorled pattern, giving it a striking appearance that resembles a living sculpture.	https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/Araucaria_en_Parque_Nacional_Conguillio.jpg/960px-Araucaria_en_Parque_Nacional_Conguillio.jpg	Full sun to partial shade	Moderate, allowing soil to dry slightly between waterings	15 to 25 feet apart	Up to 50 meters (164 feet) tall and 10-15 meters (33-49 feet) wide	Year-round	Moderate to difficult, due to its specific growing requirements	The Monkey Puzzle Tree prefers well-draining soil and requires regular watering, especially during dry periods. It is not tolerant of waterlogged conditions. Fertilize with a balanced fertilizer in the spring. Pruning should be minimal, as the tree naturally maintains its shape. This tree is hardy in USDA Zones 7-10.	f	f	f	f		evergreen, conifer, ornamental, unique	Araucariaceae	Propagation is typically done through seeds, which should be soaked in water for 24 hours before planting. Seeds should be sown in a well-draining potting mix and kept moist until germination.		flower_garden	loam
127	Black-eyed Susan	Rudbeckia hirta	Perennial	Black-eyed Susan is a flowering plant native to North America, characterized by its bright yellow petals and a dark brown center. It is known for its daisy-like appearance and is commonly used in gardens for its vibrant color and ability to attract pollinators.		Full sun to partial shade	Moderate, allow soil to dry out between waterings	12 to 18 inches apart	2 to 3 feet tall and 1 to 2 feet wide	Summer to early fall	Easy	Black-eyed Susans thrive in well-drained soil and require minimal maintenance. Deadheading spent flowers will promote further blooming. They are drought-tolerant once established and can benefit from a light fertilization in spring.	f	t	t	f		flower, perennial, garden, pollinator-friendly	Asteraceae	Can be propagated by seeds, division, or cuttings.		flower_garden	loam
128	Silver Birch	Betula pendula	Deciduous tree	The Silver Birch is a medium-sized deciduous tree known for its striking white bark and delicate, pendulous branches. It typically grows between 15 to 25 meters tall and has a slender, graceful appearance. The leaves are triangular to ovate, with serrated edges, and turn a beautiful golden yellow in autumn.	https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Betula_pendula_Finland.jpg/960px-Betula_pendula_Finland.jpg	Full sun to partial shade	Moderate; prefers well-drained soil	6 to 8 meters apart	15 to 25 meters tall, 7 to 10 meters wide	Spring to Fall	Easy	Silver Birch trees thrive in well-drained, sandy or loamy soils. They prefer full sun but can tolerate partial shade. Regular watering is necessary during dry periods, especially for young trees. Pruning should be done in late winter or early spring to maintain shape and remove any dead or diseased branches. Fertilization is generally not required unless soil quality is poor.	f	f	f	f		tree, deciduous, landscape, ornamental	Betulaceae	By seed or vegetative cuttings		flower_garden	loam
129	Bluebell	Hyacinthoides non-scripta	Bulbous perennial	Bluebells are native to the woodlands of the British Isles, characterized by their drooping, bell-shaped flowers that typically bloom in shades of blue, though they can also be found in white and pink. The flowers grow in dense clusters on slender stems, with broad, strap-like leaves that emerge from the base.	https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/British_Railways_Class_Standard_Four_No_80151_Horsted_Keynes_1.jpg/960px-British_Railways_Class_Standard_Four_No_80151_Horsted_Keynes_1.jpg	Partial shade to full shade	Moderate, allow soil to dry slightly between waterings	6-12 inches apart	12-18 inches tall	Spring	Easy	Bluebells thrive in well-drained, moist soil enriched with organic matter. They prefer a shady location under trees or in woodland gardens. After blooming, allow the foliage to die back naturally before cutting it down to provide energy for the bulbs. Avoid overwatering.	t	f	f	f		native plant, woodland, perennial, spring flowers	Asparagaceae	By bulb division or seed, typically during the summer months.		flower_garden	loam
130	Japanese Anemone	Anemone hupehensis var. japonica	Perennial	Japanese Anemone is a flowering perennial plant known for its elegant, cup-shaped flowers that bloom in late summer to fall, typically in shades of white and pink. The foliage is composed of deeply lobed, dark green leaves that create a lush backdrop for the flowers.	https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Anemone_hupehensis_var._japonica_1.jpg/960px-Anemone_hupehensis_var._japonica_1.jpg	Partial shade to full sun	Moderate, keep soil consistently moist but not waterlogged	18 to 24 inches apart	2 to 4 feet tall and 2 to 3 feet wide	Blooms from late summer to fall	Easy to moderate	Japanese Anemone prefers well-drained soil rich in organic matter. It is relatively low-maintenance; however, it benefits from regular watering during dry spells. Deadheading spent flowers can encourage further blooming. In colder climates, it may require some winter protection. It can be divided every few years to prevent overcrowding.	f	f	t	f		perennial, flowering, garden, shade	Ranunculaceae	Primarily by division of established clumps in early spring or fall, or by seed in spring.		flower_garden	loam
131	Creeping Phlox	Phlox subulata	Perennial flowering plant	Creeping Phlox is a low-growing, mat-forming perennial that produces a profusion of colorful flowers in spring. It has needle-like leaves that form dense carpets, making it an excellent ground cover. The flowers come in various colors, including pink, purple, blue, and white, attracting pollinators such as butterflies.	https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Lophospermum_erubescens_07.jpg/960px-Lophospermum_erubescens_07.jpg	Full sun to partial shade	Moderate, allow soil to dry between waterings	12 to 18 inches apart	6 to 12 inches tall, spreading up to 24 inches	Spring to early summer	Easy	Creeping Phlox thrives in well-drained soil and full sunlight. Water regularly but avoid overwatering. Prune back after flowering to maintain shape and encourage new growth. Fertilizing in early spring can enhance blooming. Mulching can help retain moisture and suppress weeds.	t	f	f	f		ground cover, perennial, flowering, drought-tolerant	Polemoniaceae	Propagate by division in spring or fall, or by stem cuttings.		flower_garden	loam
132	Weeping Cherry	Prunus subhirtella 'Pendula'	Deciduous Tree	The Weeping Cherry is a graceful, ornamental tree known for its stunning, drooping branches and beautiful pink or white flowers that bloom in early spring. It typically grows to a height of 15 to 25 feet and has a broad, rounded shape. The tree's foliage turns a lovely golden yellow in the fall, adding to its ornamental value.	https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/2018_Fort_Tryon_Park_-_weeping_cherry_tree_in_early_spring.jpg/960px-2018_Fort_Tryon_Park_-_weeping_cherry_tree_in_early_spring.jpg	Full sun to partial shade	Moderate, allowing soil to dry slightly between waterings	10 to 15 feet apart	15 to 25 feet tall and 15 to 20 feet wide	Spring	Moderate	Weeping Cherry trees thrive in well-drained soil and prefer slightly acidic to neutral pH. Prune after flowering to maintain shape and remove any dead or diseased branches. Fertilize in early spring with a balanced fertilizer to promote healthy growth. Regular watering is essential, especially during dry spells.	t	f	f	f		ornamental, flowering tree, landscape, deciduous	Rosaceae	Typically propagated by grafting or budding, though seeds can be used with lower success rates.		flower_garden	loam
133	Golden Poppy	Eschscholzia californica	Annual flowering plant	The Golden Poppy, also known as California Poppy, is a vibrant, orange-yellow flower native to the western United States and Mexico. It features cup-shaped blossoms that bloom primarily in spring and early summer, and its foliage consists of finely divided, fern-like leaves.	https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5b/GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg	Full sun to partial shade	Low; drought-tolerant once established	12 to 18 inches apart	12 to 24 inches tall	Spring to early summer	Easy	Golden Poppies thrive in well-drained soil with full sun exposure. Water sparingly, as they prefer dry conditions. They do not need much fertilization and are best left to grow naturally. Remove spent flowers to encourage additional blooms.	t	t	f	f		flower, annual, drought-tolerant, native plant	Papaveraceae	Seed propagation; direct sow seeds in fall or early spring.		flower_garden	loam
134	Himalayan Blue Poppy	Meconopsis betonicifolia	Perennial herb	The Himalayan Blue Poppy is renowned for its striking blue flowers and is native to the mountainous regions of the Himalayas. It grows to a height of about 60-90 cm (24-35 inches) and features large, cup-shaped blossoms that bloom in hues ranging from sky blue to deep blue. The foliage consists of broad, hairy leaves that form a rosette at the base.	https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Himalayan_blue_poppy_in_a_Scottish_garden.jpg/960px-Himalayan_blue_poppy_in_a_Scottish_garden.jpg	Partial shade to full shade	Consistent moisture, do not let dry out	30-45 cm (12-18 inches) apart	60-90 cm (24-35 inches) tall	Spring to early summer	Intermediate to difficult	Himalayan Blue Poppy prefers cool, moist conditions and thrives in well-drained, humus-rich soil. It is essential to provide shade, especially in hotter climates, to prevent wilting. Water regularly to keep the soil moist but avoid waterlogging. Fertilize lightly with a balanced fertilizer during the growing season. Protect from strong winds and provide support if necessary.	t	t	f	f		blue, perennial, shade, Himalayan	Papaveraceae	By seed or division of established clumps in spring or fall.		flower_garden	loam
